---
/**
 * website/src/components/CampaignSteps.astro — how it works (go-to-market plan, Task G2.2;
 * spec §6, section 3).
 *
 * **The three steps are the spec's own three**, in the spec's order and in the spec's words:
 * cheap models do the work, a premium model reviews it, and the caps keep the spend in check.
 * They are the same three on all four pages — the campaign's headline is the variant's, the
 * mechanism is the product's — so this is the one section of a campaign page whose copy is not
 * read from `website/data/landing.yaml`, and it is the section the supporting lines under the
 * four headlines describe: a reader who wants to know what the promise rests on gets it here.
 *
 * **Each step is an item of an ordered list, with its own heading and its own line.** The list
 * is the order a reader reads them in and the order they happen in, and the headings are what a
 * screen reader lists the steps by. There is no icon on top of a step (the owner's feedback of
 * 2026-10-30, finding 4): the heading already says what the step is, and a mark above it was a
 * second thing to read for one fact — three of them in a row being the reason the section read
 * as three identical cards rather than as one composition.
 *
 * **The section is as wide as the page.** Each step is a row of the band at the wide viewport —
 * the heading on the reading start in the narrower column and the line beside it in the wider
 * one, on one baseline — rather than a third of a three-column row, so the type is set at the
 * size the page's own scale gives a section rather than at the size three columns leave room
 * for. The band is a field one tone step above the ground (`--panel`, the field the homepage's
 * own stage is drawn in) and it carries no rule between the steps: the air does the separating.
 * **Its corners are rounded generously** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, D412's finding 2):
 * the panel was a hard-cornered field, and it is cut at `--radius-xl` — the site's largest radius
 * step, the panel radius the token scale draws for a panel (`src/styles/tokens.css`) — which is
 * the same corner as the closing field below it. No token is added for that: the scale has the
 * step, and the two bands are one shape. It is the one radius this file names; the hero's frame
 * and the slider's frame are controls and cards, and they stay at `--radius-lg`.
 *
 * **Its parts rise into place once.** The band is a `data-reveal-block`, so `site.js`'s own
 * entrance holds back the heading and each step's two parts while they are below the first
 * screen and shows them in reading order as the band enters — with no script nothing is ever
 * held back, and a reader who asked their system for less motion is served the section still
 * (`public/scripts/site.js`). The travel is the 24 px the owner asked the campaign's sections
 * for rather than the home page's 12 px, and it is declared inside the same `no-preference`
 * query the rest of the campaign's motion is: the reader's answer, and not a rule elsewhere
 * neutralising it.
 */
---

<section class="campaign-steps" data-section="how-it-works" data-reveal-block>
  <h2 class="campaign-steps-title display-lg">How it works</h2>
  <ol class="campaign-steps-list">
    <li class="campaign-step">
      <h3 class="campaign-step-name">Cheap models do the work</h3>
      <p class="campaign-step-line">Every run drafts on the cheap models, and each call is priced as it is made.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="campaign-step">
      <h3 class="campaign-step-name">A premium model reviews it</h3>
      <p class="campaign-step-line">The review and the fixing rounds go to the model the recipe names, so the policy is the same on every developer's machine.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="campaign-step">
      <h3 class="campaign-step-name">Caps keep spend in check</h3>
      <p class="campaign-step-line">A run stops at the cap you set, and nothing past it is spent.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2). The steps stack on a phone — the order is the list's, and a phone reads
     them one at a time — and each becomes a row of the band from the wide breakpoint, which is
     the layout the static half of `website/test/landing-pages.mjs` reads; the rendered reading
     at both widths is the owner's check (G2.5). */
  .campaign-steps {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl);
    padding-inline: var(--frame-gutter);
    border-radius: var(--radius-xl);
    background: var(--panel);
  }

  /* The section's own size, scaled with the viewport and always under the hero's headline: the
     headline runs at 5.5 vw and stops at `--font-size-4xl`, so 4.4 vw under `--font-size-3xl`
     is a step below it at every width — 20 px under a 21.4 px headline on a phone, 44 px under
     56 px at 1440. `--font-size-lg` is the floor, so the heading never falls to the body's size
     on a narrow viewport. The `.display-lg` class still carries the family, the weight and the
     tracking; this rule is the size, and `base-styles.mjs` reads the pair. */
  .campaign-steps-title {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-lg), 4.4vw, var(--font-size-3xl));
  }

  .campaign-steps-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    margin: var(--space-2xl) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* The step's name is a heading, not a card's title: it scales with the viewport too, on the
     slope below the section's heading (3.6 vw under 4.4 vw, `--font-size-2xl` under
     `--font-size-3xl` where the two stop, and the same `--font-size-lg` floor on a phone), so at
     1440 the three names are 32 px and the eye takes them in before the lines beside them. */
  .campaign-step-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-lg), 3.6vw, var(--font-size-2xl));
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The line under a name is the body's size on a phone and the body's larger step once the
     viewport has room for it, and it stays under the name at every width: it is the pair that
     makes a step a heading and a line rather than two paragraphs. */
  .campaign-step-line {
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-base), 1.9vw, var(--font-size-lg));
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    /* The phone's own block: the section's air is a step smaller at both ends, so the three
       steps and their headings fit the narrow column without the band becoming a scroll of its
       own. */
    .campaign-steps {
      padding-block: var(--space-lg);
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .campaign-steps {
      padding-block: var(--space-2xl);
    }

    .campaign-steps-list {
      gap: var(--space-2xl);
      margin-block-start: var(--space-3xl);
    }

    /* The row: the heading on the reading start and the line beside it, on one baseline, so the
       three steps read down the band rather than across it. */
    .campaign-step {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
      column-gap: var(--space-xl);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    .campaign-step-line {
      margin-block-start: 0;
    }
  }

  /* The campaign's own travel, which is the owner's 16–32 px for these four pages rather than
     the home page's 12 px, and is declared only for a reader who has not asked their system for
     less motion: `site.js` sets no `data-reveal` under `reduce` at all, and nothing here needs
     the layout's universal rule to undo it. `screen` keeps a printed page at its own place —
     `base.css` shows every held-back part at `transform: none` for print. */
  @media screen and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .campaign-steps [data-reveal]:not([data-revealed]) {
      transform: translateY(24px);
    }

    /* The row is not one of the parts that move. `site.js` marks the `li` `data-reveal` along
       with the `h3` and the `p` inside it, so the travel rule above reaches the row too — and a
       row that translates with its own parts inside it doubles the rise to 48 px, past the
       16–32 px the owner asked for. The row is therefore neutralised: it is still the observer's
       target and still the box its parts move in, and only the parts enter. The rule sits *after*
       the travel rule and outranks it — `.campaign-steps .campaign-step[data-reveal]:not(…)` is
       one class more specific than `.campaign-steps [data-reveal]:not(…)` — because order alone
       would leave the more specific travel rule winning, which is what the first cut of this
       did; `base-styles.mjs` now reads the two selectors' specificity rather than trusting the
       comment. `opacity: 1` is half of it: `base.css` hides every unrevealed part, and the row
       holding the parts must not be the thing that is hidden. */
    .campaign-steps .campaign-step[data-reveal]:not([data-revealed]) {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
